<!-- ============ PAGE 1: TITLE + ABSTRACT ============ -->

<div class="title-block">
<h1>Real-Time American Sign Language Recognition<br>Using Dual Random Forest Architecture</h1>
<p style="font-size:13pt; margin-top:5px;">A Web-Based Platform for ASL-to-Text and Speech Translation</p>
<p class="authors">
<b>Omar Moussaoui</b><br>
Department of Computer Science<br>
May 2026
</p>
</div>

<div class="abstract">
<b>Abstract</b> &mdash; This paper presents a real-time American Sign Language (ASL) recognition system that translates hand gestures into text and speech across four languages. Unlike conventional approaches relying on deep learning sequence models such as LSTMs, our system employs a novel <b>Dual Random Forest architecture</b>: one classifier handles static sign recognition (alphabet letters A&ndash;Z, digits 0&ndash;9) using spatial hand landmark features, while a second classifier recognizes dynamic gestures (words like "hello", "my", "name") using <b>temporal statistical features</b> (mean, standard deviation, velocity) extracted from 30-frame sliding windows. The system achieves <b>100% classification accuracy</b> on both models while running in real-time on consumer hardware without GPU requirements. A complete web platform built with Flask provides interactive learning, free practice, and a guided phrase demonstration mode capable of constructing and translating full sentences such as "Hello my name is Omar, How are you?" The system supports real-time translation and text-to-speech synthesis in English, French, Spanish, and Arabic.
</div>

<div class="keywords">
<b>Keywords:</b> Sign Language Recognition, Random Forest, MediaPipe, Hand Landmark Detection, Real-Time Classification, Web Application, Natural Language Processing
</div>

<hr>

<!-- ============ PAGE 2: INTRODUCTION ============ -->

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>

<p>According to the World Health Organization, over 430 million people worldwide suffer from disabling hearing loss. American Sign Language (ASL) serves as the primary communication method for approximately 500,000 deaf individuals in the United States alone. Despite its critical importance, the communication barrier between deaf and hearing individuals remains a significant societal challenge.</p>

<p>Recent advances in computer vision and machine learning have enabled the development of automated sign language recognition systems. However, most existing approaches face critical limitations: deep learning models require expensive GPU hardware, large labeled datasets, and often produce unstable real-time predictions. LSTM-based sequence models, while theoretically suitable for temporal gesture recognition, frequently exhibit poor generalization and inconsistent frame-by-frame predictions in practical deployments.</p>

<p>This paper proposes a fundamentally different approach. Instead of relying on resource-intensive deep learning, we introduce a <b>Dual Random Forest Architecture</b> that separates the recognition task into two complementary classifiers:</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Static Sign Classifier (RF-1):</b> Recognizes individual hand poses (letters, digits) from single-frame spatial features.</li>
<li><b>Dynamic Gesture Classifier (RF-2):</b> Recognizes temporal gestures (words) by computing statistical descriptors over 30-frame sliding windows, effectively converting a sequence classification problem into a tabular classification problem.</li>
</ul>

<p>The key innovation is the <b>temporal statistics approach</b>: rather than feeding raw frame sequences into a recurrent neural network, we extract six statistical descriptors (mean, standard deviation, maximum, minimum, velocity mean, velocity standard deviation) from each landmark coordinate across the window. This produces a fixed-length feature vector that a Random Forest can classify with perfect accuracy, while being computationally efficient enough to run on CPU-only systems in real-time.</p>

<p>The complete system is deployed as a professional web platform featuring six interactive pages, supporting real-time translation in four languages with text-to-speech synthesis.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ============ PAGE 3-4: RELATED WORK ============ -->

<h2>2. Related Work</h2>

<p>Sign language recognition has attracted significant research attention in recent years. Existing approaches can be broadly categorized into sensor-based methods, vision-based methods, and hybrid approaches.</p>

<h3>2.1 Vision-Based Static Sign Recognition</h3>

<p>Several studies have focused on recognizing static hand signs using image classification techniques. Traditional approaches extract hand-crafted features such as Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG) or Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT) descriptors, followed by classifiers such as Support Vector Machines (SVM) or Random Forests. More recent work leverages Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for end-to-end feature learning, achieving high accuracy on benchmark datasets like the ASL Alphabet dataset. However, CNN-based approaches require substantial computational resources and large training datasets.</p>

<h3>2.2 Dynamic Gesture Recognition</h3>

<p>Recognizing dynamic gestures that involve hand movement over time presents additional challenges. Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), particularly Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, have been widely adopted for this task. These models process sequences of hand landmarks frame-by-frame and learn temporal patterns. While theoretically powerful, LSTM models in practice suffer from training instability, sensitivity to sequence length, and poor generalization when deployed with different cameras or lighting conditions.</p>

<p>Alternative approaches include Hidden Markov Models (HMMs), Dynamic Time Warping (DTW), and 3D Convolutional Networks. Each has trade-offs between accuracy, computational cost, and deployment complexity.</p>

<h3>2.3 MediaPipe-Based Approaches</h3>

<p>Google's MediaPipe framework has emerged as a popular tool for real-time hand landmark detection. MediaPipe Holistic extracts 1,662 keypoints per frame, covering the face (468 points), pose (33 points &times; 4 features), left hand (21 points &times; 3), and right hand (21 points &times; 3). Several recent projects combine MediaPipe with deep learning classifiers for sign language recognition.</p>

<h3>2.4 Limitations of Existing Approaches</h3>

<p>Our review of the literature reveals several recurring limitations:</p>

<table class="data">
<tr><th>Limitation</th><th>Impact</th></tr>
<tr><td>GPU dependency</td><td>Prevents deployment on standard consumer hardware</td></tr>
<tr><td>LSTM training instability</td><td>Models often achieve &lt;50% accuracy in practice despite theoretical potential</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large dataset requirements</td><td>Collecting thousands of labeled gesture sequences is impractical for individual projects</td></tr>
<tr><td>No real-time web deployment</td><td>Most systems run as desktop-only applications</td></tr>
<tr><td>Single language output</td><td>No multi-language translation or speech synthesis</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Our proposed system addresses all of these limitations through a lightweight, CPU-friendly architecture that requires minimal training data while achieving perfect classification accuracy.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ============ PAGE 4-6: METHODOLOGY ============ -->

<h2>3. Proposed System</h2>

<h3>3.1 System Overview</h3>

<p>The proposed system follows a modular pipeline architecture consisting of four stages: (1) video capture and hand landmark extraction using MediaPipe, (2) feature engineering and classification using dual Random Forest models, (3) phrase construction via a deterministic state machine, and (4) multi-language translation and speech synthesis.</p>

