<h3>3.2 Hand Landmark Extraction</h3>

<p>We use MediaPipe Holistic to extract hand landmarks in real-time. For the static classifier (RF-1), we extract 21 hand landmarks per hand, each with (x, y) coordinates normalized relative to the bounding box, yielding a 42-dimensional feature vector. For the dynamic classifier (RF-2), we extract both left and right hand landmarks with (x, y, z) coordinates, producing a 126-dimensional vector per frame (21 landmarks &times; 3 coordinates &times; 2 hands).</p>

<h3>3.3 Static Sign Classification (RF-1)</h3>

<p>The first Random Forest model classifies static hand poses from single frames. For each detected hand, we compute the minimum x and y coordinates of all 21 landmarks, then normalize each landmark by subtracting these minima. This produces a translation-invariant 42-dimensional feature vector:</p>

<p style="text-align:center; font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-style: italic;">
f<sub>static</sub> = [x<sub>1</sub> &minus; x<sub>min</sub>, y<sub>1</sub> &minus; y<sub>min</sub>, ..., x<sub>21</sub> &minus; x<sub>min</sub>, y<sub>21</sub> &minus; y<sub>min</sub>]
</p>

<p>The model was trained on 3,825 samples across 37 classes (25 letters, 10 digits, space, period) using 300 images per class collected via webcam. We use a Random Forest with 100 estimators, achieving 100% test accuracy.</p>

<h3>3.4 Dynamic Gesture Classification (RF-2)</h3>

<p>The key innovation of this work is the temporal statistics approach for dynamic gesture recognition. Instead of using sequence models, we compute statistical descriptors over a sliding window of 30 frames:</p>

<p>Given a sequence S = {s<sub>1</sub>, s<sub>2</sub>, ..., s<sub>30</sub>} where each s<sub>t</sub> &isin; R<sup>126</sup>, we compute:</p>

<table class="data">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Formula</th><th>Dimensionality</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mean</td><td>&mu; = (1/T) &Sigma; s<sub>t</sub></td><td>126</td></tr>
<tr><td>Std. Deviation</td><td>&sigma; = sqrt((1/T) &Sigma; (s<sub>t</sub> &minus; &mu;)&sup2;)</td><td>126</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum</td><td>max(s<sub>1</sub>, ..., s<sub>T</sub>)</td><td>126</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum</td><td>min(s<sub>1</sub>, ..., s<sub>T</sub>)</td><td>126</td></tr>
<tr><td>Velocity Mean</td><td>(1/(T-1)) &Sigma; |s<sub>t+1</sub> &minus; s<sub>t</sub>|</td><td>126</td></tr>
<tr><td>Velocity Std</td><td>std(s<sub>t+1</sub> &minus; s<sub>t</sub>)</td><td>126</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The concatenation of these six descriptors produces a 756-dimensional feature vector (126 &times; 6). This approach captures both the spatial configuration of the hand (via mean, max, min) and the temporal dynamics of the gesture (via standard deviation and velocity features). A Random Forest with 200 estimators classifies this vector with 100% test accuracy and 99.0% cross-validation accuracy across 7 classes.</p>

<h3>3.5 State Machine for Phrase Construction</h3>

<p>The system uses a three-phase deterministic state machine to guide users through constructing the phrase "Hello my name is Omar, How are you?":</p>

<table class="data">
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Mode</th><th>Steps</th><th>Model</th></tr>
<tr><td>G1</td><td>Dynamic Gesture</td><td>hello &rarr; my &rarr; name &rarr; is</td><td>RF-2</td></tr>
<tr><td>LETTER</td><td>Static Spelling</td><td>H &rarr; a &rarr; i &rarr; t &rarr; a &rarr; m</td><td>RF-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>G2</td><td>Dynamic Gesture</td><td>how(+are) &rarr; you</td><td>RF-2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Each step requires stabilization (3 consecutive matching predictions for gestures, 3 for letters) followed by a cooldown period to prevent double detection. The "how" gesture automatically outputs "How are" to simplify the user experience.</p>

