<!-- ============ PAGE 9: DISCUSSION ============ -->

<h2>7. Discussion</h2>

<h3>7.1 Why Temporal Statistics Outperform LSTM</h3>

<p>The success of the temporal statistics approach can be explained by several factors:</p>

<ol>
<li><b>Feature interpretability:</b> Statistical descriptors explicitly capture the properties that distinguish gestures. A waving "hello" produces high standard deviation in hand x-coordinates, while a static "is" sign produces low standard deviation.</li>
<li><b>Fixed-length representation:</b> By summarizing variable-length sequences into fixed-length vectors, we eliminate sequence padding challenges and vanishing gradients.</li>
<li><b>Data efficiency:</b> Random Forests require significantly fewer training samples. With only 30 sequences per class, we achieve perfect classification.</li>
<li><b>Robustness:</b> Statistical features are inherently more robust to minor variations in gesture speed and amplitude.</li>
<li><b>Computational efficiency:</b> RF inference is a series of if-else comparisons, orders of magnitude faster than LSTM matrix multiplications. No GPU needed.</li>
</ol>

<h3>7.2 Training Pipeline Analysis</h3>

<table class="data">
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Script</th><th>Duration</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Collect letter images</td><td>collectImgs.py</td><td>~20 min</td><td>11,100 images</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Extract hand features</td><td>createDataset.py</td><td>~3 min</td><td>data.pickle</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Train letter RF</td><td>trainClassifier.py</td><td>&lt;2 sec</td><td>model.p (100%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Collect gesture sequences</td><td>collect_gestures.py</td><td>~10 min</td><td>6,300 .npy files</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Train gesture RF</td><td>train_gesture_rf.py</td><td>&lt;3 sec</td><td>gesture_rf.p (100%)</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Total training time: under 40 minutes on CPU only, compared to hours for deep learning.</p>

<h3>7.3 Stabilization Mechanism</h3>

<p>To prevent false detections, we implemented dual stabilization: each sign must be predicted 3 consecutive times before acceptance. After acceptance, a cooldown period (1.5s for gestures, 0.8s for letters) prevents double detection. This ensures professional-grade stability during demonstrations.</p>

<h3>7.4 Limitations</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Vocabulary scalability:</b> 7 dynamic gestures currently. Scaling may require hierarchical classification or PCA.</li>
<li><b>User dependency:</b> Models trained on single user data. Transfer learning could help.</li>
<li><b>Lighting sensitivity:</b> MediaPipe degrades in poor lighting or partial occlusion.</li>
<li><b>Camera orientation:</b> Training data must match deployment camera orientation.</li>
<li><b>Fixed phrase order:</b> State machine enforces predetermined sequence.</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ============ PAGE 10: CONCLUSION ============ -->

<h2>8. Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<p>This paper presented a novel approach to real-time American Sign Language recognition using a Dual Random Forest architecture. By replacing unstable LSTM models with temporal statistical features computed over 30-frame sliding windows, we achieved 100% classification accuracy on both static sign recognition (37 classes) and dynamic gesture recognition (7 classes).</p>

<p>The system is deployed as a professional web platform with Flask, featuring six pages: home, learning dashboard, individual lessons with camera validation, free practice, and guided phrase demonstration. It supports real-time translation and text-to-speech in English, French, Spanish, and Arabic.</p>

<p>The key contributions of this work are:</p>

<ol>
<li>A <b>temporal statistics method</b> converting sequence classification into tabular classification, enabling Random Forests to achieve perfect accuracy with only 30 training samples per class.</li>
<li>A <b>three-phase state machine</b> combining gesture recognition with letter spelling to construct complete phrases.</li>
<li>A <b>complete web-based learning platform</b> with guided lessons, progress tracking, and phrase demonstration.</li>
<li><b>Multi-language translation and speech synthesis</b> integrated into real-time recognition.</li>
</ol>

<h3>8.1 Future Work</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Vocabulary expansion:</b> Extending from 7 to 50+ common ASL phrases.</li>
<li><b>User-independent recognition:</b> Transfer learning for cross-user generalization.</li>
<li><b>Mobile deployment:</b> Android/iOS via TensorFlow Lite and MediaPipe mobile SDK.</li>
<li><b>Two-handed signs:</b> Better spatial relationship features between both hands.</li>
<li><b>Continuous recognition:</b> Free-form sentence builder replacing fixed state machine.</li>
<li><b>Cloud deployment:</b> AWS/GCP hosting for remote access without local installation.</li>
</ul>

